 means saruman they  prepare for battle and the audience is  misdirected right along with them  then a wizard appears with his face  hidden by blinding light  only to resolve into gandalf while many  fans love character resurrections  just as many think they're terrible for  a recent example consider emperor  palpatine's resurrection in star wars  the rise of skywalker in the original  trilogy palpatine first appears in the  empire strikes back as the shadowy  puppet master of the galactic empire  before appearing in the flesh in return  of the jedi  though he attempts to bring luke  skywalker over to the dark side he is  unsuccessful and gets thrown down a  shaft by darth vader for his trouble  that seemed like the end of it and it  was until the first rise of skywalker  trailer closed with luke's words  no one's ever really gone and the  emperor's familiar raspy laugh  as the release date approached fans  waited expectantly to see how he'd come  back in a series that had mostly avoided  reviving characters from the dead  in the end the only explanation we get  amounts to somehow palpatine survived  and has returned  many fans were unsurprisingly displeased  but his return to the living didn't  annoy fans as much as what he does when  he gets there  he reveals that he is rey's grandfather  a twist so bizarre many fans had  predicted it entirely as a joke  but in the end only some people found it  amusing  unlike most characters on our list this  one had to wait many years and many  movies before he made his dramatic  return  in captain america the first avenger the  red skull is a nazi officer whose  experiments gave him superhuman strength  but they also flayed the skin from his  face  he uses the mystical tesseract to power  his collection of space-age weapons  until cap knocks it out of its casing  and the red skull tries to hold it in  his bare hands which seemingly  disintegrates him  a year later in the avengers we learned  that the tesseract is  actually the space stone and that it  works by opening wormholes in space  comics savvy viewers immediately guessed  that the red skull hadn't been killed  just sent  elsewhere it took six years but those  guesses were confirmed in avengers  infinity war when the murderous thanos  arrives on the planet vormir  looking for the soul stone he's greeted  by a hooded figure who reveals a  familiar  red face the red skull explains that the  tesseract transported him there and  since he didn't love anything or anyone  enough to sacrifice to the stone  he guides others to a treasure he can  never possess  no genre loves bringing characters back  from the dead as much as slasher horror  does  this tradition goes all the way back to  1978's halloween  and its iconic villain michael myers the  classic flick leaves his death uncertain  with a final scene that reveals his  corpse has disappeared after being shot  multiple times  sure enough he's back to kill again in  halloween two and later sequels find  more and more outlandish ways to bring  him back none of them however  quite top his baffling return in  halloween resurrection  the previous movie halloween h2o 20  years later ends with laurie strode  straight up hacking meyer's head off  so hard it rolls across the ground how  do you come back from that  well according to resurrection that  beheaded body isn't michael at  all oh my god she killed the wrong  person  a flashback explains he switched clothes  with a paramedic before laurie caught up  with him  it works better than it has any right to  the fake michael grabs at his mask at  one point as if confused why he's  wearing it  but there are a lot of things this  doesn't explain for starters why would  the paramedic lunge at lorry  as soon as he wakes up in the van and  why through the whole thing but  especially when laurie's coming at him  with an  axe does he never think to just take the  mask off to reveal that he's actually a  middle-aged man with a mustache  in the original nightmare on elm street  freddy krueger returns from the grave to  torment the children of the people who  killed him  but the series is all about finding new  ways to make sure he stays dead for good  and none of them seem to work  in nightmare on elm street 3 dream  warriors neil gordon the psychiatrist  treating freddie's current round of  victims  learns he can put the killer away for  good by dousing his bones in holy water  it seems like our long cinematic  nightmare is over at least until  nightmare on elm street  for the dream master as the film starts  a character dreams that his dog is  taking a fiery leak  then a pit opens up revealing freddie's  remains  his bones start reconnecting his scarred  flesh reforms  even his signature hat is back and  that's all the explanation we get  well except for this you shouldn't have  buried me  i'm not dead freddy's creator wes craven  came up with a much more elegant  solution when he returned to the  franchise in new nightmare  freddy's still dead but he turns out to  be much more than just a fictional  character  he's an ancient evil force now free to  enter our reality he targets original  nightmare star heather langenkamp and  the rest of the cast and crew before  heather finally banishes him to the  world of fiction once again  serial killer charles lee ray first  cheats death in child's play by using a  voodoo ritual he learned from an old  accomplice  transferring his soul into a doll the  ending of this film practically dares  any sequel maker to find a way to bring  chucky back he ends up burned to a crisp  then he has an  arm a leg and his head shot off finally  he takes a bullet to the heart  yet bring him back they did child's play  too is the most  inexplicable return in the series it  opens with the play pals toy company  cleaning up chucky's severed head and  building a new doll around it  why would they do this according to a  playpal's executive it's to prove that  no one at the factory tampered with 